1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:05,596
All they wanted was Caesar is Lord and a
pinch of incense.

2
00:00:05,916 --> 00:00:07,665
Do that, and he walks home.

3
00:00:08,435 --> 00:00:09,395
He wouldn't.

4
00:00:10,765 --> 00:00:13,709
An old man stands in the stadium at
Smyrna.

5
00:00:14,029 --> 00:00:17,485
The crowd screams for him, and the
proconsul waits.

6
00:00:18,255 --> 00:00:20,175
I won't show you the graphic part.

7
00:00:20,495 --> 00:00:23,973
What matters is what he said before they
lit the fire.

8
00:00:24,743 --> 00:00:30,214
His name was Polycarp, and by his own
count he had served Christ eighty-six

9
00:00:30,214 --> 00:00:30,652
years.

10
00:00:31,422 --> 00:00:36,307
This is the story of those years, and the
sentence that cost him his life.

11
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:46,712
Polycarp lived in Smyrna, in what is now
Turkey.

12
00:00:47,032 --> 00:00:48,440
A busy port city.

13
00:00:48,760 --> 00:00:51,170
Ships, temples, markets, soldiers.

14
00:00:51,940 --> 00:00:54,095
He was a pastor there, a bishop.

15
00:00:54,415 --> 00:00:56,612
But before that, he was a student.

16
00:00:57,382 --> 00:01:03,184
A man named Irenaeus heard Polycarp preach
as a boy, and he wrote that Polycarp had

17
00:01:03,184 --> 00:01:05,212
talked with the Apostle John.

18
00:01:05,532 --> 00:01:09,414
The same John who leaned on Jesus at the
last supper.

19
00:01:10,184 --> 00:01:12,360
Beloved, let us love one another.

20
00:01:13,730 --> 00:01:14,968
Think about that.

21
00:01:15,288 --> 00:01:17,314
Polycarp is a living link.

22
00:01:17,634 --> 00:01:19,896
Most of the apostles are gone now.

23
00:01:20,216 --> 00:01:24,568
He is one of the last who can say, I heard
it from someone who was there.

24
00:01:25,338 --> 00:01:26,703
And the crowd knows it.

25
00:01:28,895 --> 00:01:35,466
A persecution breaks out in Smyrna, most
historians say around AD one fifty-five.

26
00:01:35,786 --> 00:01:37,557
Christians are arrested.

27
00:01:37,877 --> 00:01:39,050
Some are killed.

28
00:01:39,820 --> 00:01:44,449
Then the crowd in the stadium starts to
shout, Away with the atheists!

29
00:01:44,769 --> 00:01:46,177
Find Polycarp!

30
00:01:46,947 --> 00:01:49,294
Polycarp wants to stay in the city.

31
00:01:49,614 --> 00:01:51,833
His friends talk him into leaving.

32
00:01:52,153 --> 00:01:56,846
He goes to a small farm outside town, and
he does what he always did.

33
00:01:57,166 --> 00:01:58,169
He prays.

34
00:01:58,489 --> 00:01:59,683
For the churches.

35
00:02:00,003 --> 00:02:01,049
For the world.

36
00:02:01,369 --> 00:02:03,182
For the people hunting him.

37
00:02:03,952 --> 00:02:07,216
Three days before his arrest, he sees a
vision.

38
00:02:07,536 --> 00:02:10,160
The pillow under his head is on fire.

39
00:02:10,930 --> 00:02:14,877
He turns to his friends and says, I must
be burned alive.

40
00:02:15,647 --> 00:02:18,548
He doesn't say it like a man who is
guessing.

41
00:02:18,868 --> 00:02:21,641
He says it like a man who has been told.

42
00:02:23,833 --> 00:02:26,094
The men hunting him get closer.

43
00:02:26,414 --> 00:02:28,142
He moves to another farm.

44
00:02:28,462 --> 00:02:29,444
They follow.

45
00:02:30,214 --> 00:02:35,113
They can't find him, so they take two
young slaves from his own household and

46
00:02:35,113 --> 00:02:37,467
torture them until one of them talks.

47
00:02:38,237 --> 00:02:41,885
Polycarp was betrayed by people who ate at
his table.

48
00:02:42,655 --> 00:02:45,514
The police captain is a man named Herod.

49
00:02:45,834 --> 00:02:51,654
On a Friday, around suppertime, Herod
rides out with armed men, as if chasing a

50
00:02:51,654 --> 00:02:52,170
robber.

51
00:02:52,940 --> 00:02:56,673
They find Polycarp in an upstairs room,
lying down.

52
00:02:56,993 --> 00:02:58,358
He could slip away.

53
00:02:58,678 --> 00:02:59,660
He doesn't.

54
00:03:01,130 --> 00:03:03,647
He says, God's will be done.

55
00:03:05,247 --> 00:03:09,066
Then he comes down and talks with the men
who came to take him.

56
00:03:09,836 --> 00:03:13,164
And here is the detail that gets me every
time.

57
00:03:13,484 --> 00:03:15,340
He sees they are hungry.

58
00:03:15,660 --> 00:03:18,113
So he orders food and drink for them.

59
00:03:18,433 --> 00:03:21,718
The old man they are about to arrest feeds
them first.

60
00:03:22,488 --> 00:03:24,579
Then he asks for one hour to pray.

61
00:03:24,899 --> 00:03:26,030
They say yes.

62
00:03:26,350 --> 00:03:28,014
He prays for two hours.

63
00:03:28,334 --> 00:03:33,414
For everyone he has ever known, great and
small, and for the whole church in the

64
00:03:33,414 --> 00:03:33,795
world.

65
00:03:34,565 --> 00:03:38,618
Some of the men watching start to regret
what they came to do.

66
00:03:40,810 --> 00:03:44,116
They set him on a donkey and take him
toward the city.

67
00:03:44,886 --> 00:03:49,708
On the road, Herod and his father meet
them, and take him up into their carriage.

68
00:03:50,028 --> 00:03:51,777
They are not cruel at first.

69
00:03:52,097 --> 00:03:57,107
What harm is it, they say, to say Lord
Caesar, and offer a little incense, and

70
00:03:57,107 --> 00:03:58,006
save yourself?

71
00:03:58,776 --> 00:04:00,398
Polycarp stays quiet.

72
00:04:00,718 --> 00:04:01,934
They keep pushing.

73
00:04:02,254 --> 00:04:06,094
Finally he says, I am not going to do what
you advise me.

74
00:04:06,864 --> 00:04:08,229
Now they turn hard.

75
00:04:08,549 --> 00:04:12,517
They shove him out of the carriage so fast
he scrapes his shin.

76
00:04:12,837 --> 00:04:16,208
He gets up and keeps walking, as if
nothing happened.

77
00:04:16,978 --> 00:04:20,775
And as he comes into the stadium, a voice
comes from heaven.

78
00:04:21,095 --> 00:04:23,975
Be strong, Polycarp, and play the man.

79
00:04:24,745 --> 00:04:27,412
That is what the believers there said they
heard.

80
00:04:29,604 --> 00:04:31,460
The proconsul looks at him.

81
00:04:31,780 --> 00:04:32,804
An old man.

82
00:04:33,124 --> 00:04:34,425
This should be easy.

83
00:04:35,195 --> 00:04:37,563
He says, Have respect for your age.

84
00:04:37,883 --> 00:04:39,867
Swear by the fortune of Caesar.

85
00:04:40,187 --> 00:04:41,019
Repent.

86
00:04:41,339 --> 00:04:43,259
Say, Away with the atheists.

87
00:04:44,029 --> 00:04:45,352
Here is the trap.

88
00:04:45,672 --> 00:04:50,522
In the Roman world, atheist meant
Christian, because Christians would not

89
00:04:50,522 --> 00:04:52,050
worship the Roman gods.

90
00:04:52,370 --> 00:04:56,317
The proconsul isn't asking Polycarp to
curse himself.

91
00:04:56,637 --> 00:04:59,624
He is asking him to curse his own people.

92
00:05:00,394 --> 00:05:03,636
Polycarp looks at the crowd screaming for
his blood.

93
00:05:03,956 --> 00:05:08,628
He waves his hand toward them, and he
says, Away with the atheists.

94
00:05:09,398 --> 00:05:10,892
He gives the word back.

95
00:05:11,662 --> 00:05:13,752
The proconsul tries again.

96
00:05:14,072 --> 00:05:16,142
Swear, and I will set you free.

97
00:05:16,462 --> 00:05:17,571
Curse Christ.

98
00:05:19,763 --> 00:05:25,026
And Polycarp says, Eighty and six years
have I served Him, and He hath done me no

99
00:05:25,026 --> 00:05:25,416
wrong.

100
00:05:25,736 --> 00:05:28,872
How can I blaspheme my King and my
Saviour?

101
00:05:29,642 --> 00:05:30,943
Eighty-six years.

102
00:05:31,263 --> 00:05:33,653
Through good emperors and bad ones.

103
00:05:33,973 --> 00:05:36,490
Through peace and through persecution.

104
00:05:36,810 --> 00:05:40,266
Through the death of friends, and the
death of apostles.

105
00:05:40,586 --> 00:05:43,935
And in all that time, Christ has done him
no wrong.

106
00:05:44,705 --> 00:05:46,796
The proconsul tries again.

107
00:05:47,116 --> 00:05:48,631
I have wild beasts.

108
00:05:48,951 --> 00:05:50,444
I will throw you to them.

109
00:05:51,214 --> 00:05:52,921
Polycarp says, Call them.

110
00:05:53,691 --> 00:05:55,397
Then I will have you burned.

111
00:05:56,167 --> 00:06:00,242
You threaten me with a fire that burns for
an hour and is put out.

112
00:06:00,562 --> 00:06:03,783
You do not know the fire of the coming
judgment.

113
00:06:04,103 --> 00:06:05,319
Why are you waiting?

114
00:06:05,639 --> 00:06:07,069
Bring what you will.

115
00:06:07,839 --> 00:06:12,831
The herald walks into the arena and shouts
three times, Polycarp has confessed

116
00:06:12,831 --> 00:06:14,495
himself to be a Christian.

117
00:06:15,265 --> 00:06:19,083
And the crowd that wanted a show starts
screaming for the fire.

118
00:06:21,275 --> 00:06:24,731
They gather wood from the workshops and
the public baths.

119
00:06:25,051 --> 00:06:29,873
They are about to nail him to the stake,
and he says, Leave me as I am.

120
00:06:30,193 --> 00:06:35,051
He who gives me strength to endure the
fire will let me stand without moving,

121
00:06:35,051 --> 00:06:36,187
without the nails.

122
00:06:36,957 --> 00:06:38,195
So they tie him.

123
00:06:38,515 --> 00:06:40,136
Hands behind his back.

124
00:06:40,456 --> 00:06:42,120
He looks up and prays.

125
00:06:42,440 --> 00:06:46,301
He thanks God for counting him worthy to
share the cup of Christ.

126
00:06:46,621 --> 00:06:48,157
And he says amen.

127
00:06:48,927 --> 00:06:50,442
Then they light the fire.

128
00:06:51,212 --> 00:06:53,388
And the account says something strange.

129
00:06:53,708 --> 00:06:56,823
The flame does not touch him the way fire
should.

130
00:06:57,143 --> 00:07:00,535
It bends around him like a sail filled
with wind.

131
00:07:00,855 --> 00:07:06,031
Those who watched said he looked like
bread baking, or gold in the furnace, and

132
00:07:06,031 --> 00:07:08,193
a sweet smell came, like incense.

133
00:07:08,963 --> 00:07:12,334
The men tending the fire call for an
executioner.

134
00:07:13,104 --> 00:07:14,747
And here is where I stop.

135
00:07:15,067 --> 00:07:17,115
I won't describe what came next.

136
00:07:17,435 --> 00:07:18,629
You don't need it.

137
00:07:19,399 --> 00:07:22,023
Polycarp had already given his answer.

138
00:07:22,343 --> 00:07:23,901
They could end his life.

139
00:07:24,221 --> 00:07:26,311
They could not take back the sentence.

140
00:07:28,503 --> 00:07:33,021
The church in Smyrna wrote down what
happened and sent it to the other

141
00:07:33,021 --> 00:07:33,602
churches.

142
00:07:33,922 --> 00:07:38,299
They wanted everyone to know what
faithfulness looks like when it costs

143
00:07:38,299 --> 00:07:38,978
everything.

144
00:07:39,748 --> 00:07:42,542
Ask yourself why a story like this lasts.

145
00:07:42,862 --> 00:07:46,297
People don't die for something they only
half believe.

146
00:07:46,617 --> 00:07:52,867
An old man doesn't walk into a stadium and
refuse one pinch of incense over a story

147
00:07:52,867 --> 00:07:54,148
somebody made up.

148
00:07:54,468 --> 00:07:56,196
Polycarp knew Jesus.

149
00:07:56,516 --> 00:07:58,969
He had followed Him for eighty-six years.

150
00:07:59,739 --> 00:08:04,070
And the day is coming when some of us will
be tested the way he was.

151
00:08:04,390 --> 00:08:06,032
It may not be a stadium.

152
00:08:06,352 --> 00:08:11,448
It could be at work, in your own family,
or under a law that tells you to keep

153
00:08:11,448 --> 00:08:12,624
quiet about Jesus.

154
00:08:12,944 --> 00:08:16,166
When following Him costs you something,
will you stand?

155
00:08:16,486 --> 00:08:19,195
Or will you walk away to save your own
life?

156
00:08:19,965 --> 00:08:22,610
Jesus answered that question Himself.

157
00:08:22,930 --> 00:08:26,685
Matthew sixteen, verses twenty-five and
twenty-six.

158
00:08:27,455 --> 00:08:32,568
For whosoever will save his life shall
lose it: and whosoever will lose his life

159
00:08:32,568 --> 00:08:37,745
for my sake shall find it. For what is a
man profited, if he shall gain the whole

160
00:08:37,745 --> 00:08:42,858
world, and lose his own soul? or what
shall a man give in exchange for his soul?

161
00:08:44,228 --> 00:08:49,625
Polycarp could stand at the end because he
had walked with Jesus every day before it.

162
00:08:49,945 --> 00:08:52,014
That walk starts with repentance.

163
00:08:52,334 --> 00:08:56,836
You stop running your own life, you turn
from your sin, and you turn to Him.

164
00:08:57,156 --> 00:08:58,969
Acts three, verse nineteen.

165
00:08:59,739 --> 00:09:04,684
Repent ye therefore, and be converted,
that your sins may be blotted out, when

166
00:09:04,684 --> 00:09:08,806
the times of refreshing shall come from
the presence of the Lord;

167
00:09:10,176 --> 00:09:14,464
If you have never done that, you can do it
right now, right where you are.

168
00:09:14,784 --> 00:09:16,426
Talk to Him like this.

169
00:09:17,196 --> 00:09:21,996
Lord Jesus, I have been living my own way,
and I am sorry.

170
00:09:22,546 --> 00:09:25,704
I turn from my sin, and I turn to You.

171
00:09:26,254 --> 00:09:29,603
I believe You died for me and rose again.

172
00:09:30,153 --> 00:09:32,692
Be my Lord and my Saviour.

173
00:09:33,242 --> 00:09:36,911
Give me a faith that stands when it costs
me something.

174
00:09:37,461 --> 00:09:39,680
In Jesus' name I pray.

175
00:09:40,230 --> 00:09:41,190
Amen.

176
00:09:42,790 --> 00:09:45,072
Somebody is watching how you live.

177
00:09:45,392 --> 00:09:46,203
A son.

178
00:09:46,523 --> 00:09:47,568
A coworker.

179
00:09:47,888 --> 00:09:48,742
A neighbor.

180
00:09:49,062 --> 00:09:52,176
Let them see someone who belongs to Jesus.

181
00:09:52,946 --> 00:09:55,634
If this reached you, type amen below.

182
00:09:55,954 --> 00:10:00,776
And ask yourself the question the old man
answered with his whole life.

183
00:10:01,096 --> 00:10:02,930
Who's watching how you live?
